+++ b/contrib/pgvector/bench/README.md
@@ -37,6 +37,78 @@ METRICS=cosine ROWS=100000 DIMS=384 LISTS=100 PROBES_LIST="1 4 10 32 100" \
   bench/run_ivfflat_benchmark.sh
 ```
 
+Measure IVFFlat rescan memory in one backend:
+
+```bash
+ITEM_ROWS=1000 DIMS=128 RESCANS=50000 LISTS=20 PROBES=1 \
+  bench/run_ivfflat_rescan_memory.sh
+```
+
+Compare high-probes query sort settings with five ABBA-ordered repetitions and
+verify exact top-10 equivalence:
+
+```bash
+PGHOST=127.0.0.1 PGPORT=22301 PGUSER=otbtest DBNAME=ivfflat_test \
+  WORK_MEM=4MB QUERY_WORK_MEM=64MB PROBES=1000 REPETITIONS=5 \
+  bench/run_ivfflat_high_probes.sh
+```
+
+The dataset must contain `profile_items(id, embedding)` and
+`profile_queries(id, embedding)`, with 100,000 items, 200 queries, and a
+Cosine IVFFlat index named `profile_items_cos_idx` built with `lists=1000`.
+The harness records complete `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` plans and an
+`aggregate.csv` file. Its correctness pass compares the full-probes IVFFlat
+top-10 sets against an exact sequential-scan reference.
+
+For a controlled compiler-vectorization ablation, a database administrator can
+alternate two previously built `vector.so` files in the same test cluster:
+
+```bash
+SCALAR_LIBRARY=/path/to/scalar/vector.so \
+VECTORIZED_LIBRARY=/path/to/vectorized/vector.so \
+ALLOW_LIBRARY_SWAP=true REPETITIONS=5 \
+  bench/run_ivfflat_simd_ablation.sh
+```
+
+This administrative harness temporarily replaces the installed `vector.so`
+for new benchmark backends and restores the original file on exit. Run it only
+on an isolated test cluster after verifying `ACTIVE_LIBRARY`; do not use it on
+a production server.
+
+On Linux, capture a CPU profile of the same high-probes workload with:
+
+```bash
+PGHOST=127.0.0.1 PGPORT=22301 PGUSER=otbtest DBNAME=ivfflat_test \
+  WORK_MEM=4MB QUERY_WORK_MEM=0 PROBES=1000 \
+  bench/run_ivfflat_perf_profile.sh
+```
+
+The profiling harness requires `perf` permission for the query backend. It
+stores the complete plan, `perf.data`, symbol reports, and unfolded stack data
+under a timestamped result directory.
+
+For OpenTenBase, connect directly to the Datanode that executes the scan so
+the plan exposes the inner index loops and `/proc` samples the correct backend.
+Create the extension through the Coordinator first, then use:
+
+```bash
+PGHOST=127.0.0.1 PGPORT=22301 PGUSER=otbtest DBNAME=ivfflat_test \
+  CREATE_EXTENSION=false OPENTENBASE_CN_PORT=22201 RESCANS=1000 \
+  bench/run_ivfflat_rescan_memory.sh
+```
+
+When `OPENTENBASE_CN_PORT` is set, the harness creates replicated benchmark
+tables through that Coordinator, samples the backend reached through `PGPORT`,
+and removes the benchmark tables through the Coordinator on exit. Do not run
+multiple external-setup instances concurrently in the same database.
+
+For a short smoke run, reduce `RESCANS` to `1000`. The rescan harness uses
+temporary tables so the indexed nested-loop work stays in the backend selected
+by `PGHOST`, `PGPORT`, and `PGUSER`. It samples that backend's Linux `/proc`
+memory counters while a single `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` query is running. The command
+fails unless the plan proves both an IVFFlat index scan and more than one index
+loop.
+
 Load the recommendation helper:
 
 ```bash
@@ -88,6 +160,28 @@ bench/results/ivfflat_benchmark_<timestamp>_plans.txt
 | `OUTPUT` | auto-generated | CSV output path |
 | `PLAN_OUTPUT` | derived from `OUTPUT` | EXPLAIN plan output path |
 
+The rescan memory harness additionally accepts `CREATE_EXTENSION`,
+`OPENTENBASE_CN_PORT`, `ITEM_ROWS`, `RESCANS`,
+`SAMPLE_INTERVAL`, `STARTUP_DELAY`, `RUN_ID`, `RESULT_DIR`, `SAMPLES_OUTPUT`,
+and `SUMMARY_OUTPUT`. Its defaults are intended for development evidence, not
+as a universal production workload.
+
+## Query sort memory
+
+IVFFlat sorts candidate tuples before returning ordered results. Large `probes`
+values can spill this sort to temporary files when `work_mem` is small. Set
+`ivfflat.query_work_mem` for a session or transaction to give only IVFFlat
+query sorts a larger budget:
+
+```sql
+SET LOCAL ivfflat.query_work_mem = '64MB';
+```
+
+The default is `0`, which preserves the standard behavior and uses `work_mem`.
+Each active IVFFlat scan can consume up to the configured amount, so increase
+it only after checking concurrent scan counts and `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)`
+temporary-block usage.
+
 ## Metrics
 
 The report contains:
@@ -97,6 +191,14 @@ The report contains:
 - `recall_at_k`: average overlap between exact top-k results and IVFFlat top-k
 - `exact_avg_latency_ms`: average exact scan latency with index scans disabled
 
+The rescan harness writes three separate evidence files:
+
+- `*_rss.csv`: time-series `VmRSS`, `RssAnon`, and `VmSize` readings for the
+  query backend
+- `*_summary.csv`: run parameters, RSS start/peak/end values, process status,
+  IVFFlat plan status, and observed index loop count
+- `*_plan.txt`: complete setup output and `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` plan
+
 Recall is calculated as:
 
 ```text
@@ -135,3 +237,7 @@ Heuristic results are starting points, not production guarantees.
   for DML statements in distributed execution.
 - Use this benchmark to compare commits, parameter settings, and hardware. Do
   not use a single run as a universal performance claim.
+- RSS is an operating-system observation and may include allocator retention.
+  Compare repeated baseline and patched runs on the same host, and use the RSS
+  growth trend together with the plan loop count rather than interpreting one
+  endpoint in isolation.
diff --git a/contrib/pgvector/bench/ivfflat_rescan_memory.sql b/contrib/pgvector/bench/ivfflat_rescan_memory.sql
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..1af641f7f3dde23dc7780d247344a8a809267093
--- /dev/null
+++ b/contrib/pgvector/bench/ivfflat_rescan_memory.sql
@@ -0,0 +1,63 @@
+\set ON_ERROR_STOP on
+\pset pager off
+
+\if :external_setup
+SELECT extversion AS vector_version
+FROM pg_extension
+WHERE extname = 'vector';
+\else
+\if :create_extension
+CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS vector;
+\else
+SELECT extversion AS vector_version
+FROM pg_extension
+WHERE extname = 'vector';
+\endif
+
+CREATE TEMP TABLE rescan_items (
+    id integer PRIMARY KEY,
+    embedding vector(__DIMS__)
+);
+
+CREATE TEMP TABLE rescan_queries (
+    id integer PRIMARY KEY,
+    embedding vector(__DIMS__)
+);
+
+\copy rescan_items(id, embedding) FROM '__ITEMS_FILE__' WITH (FORMAT csv)
+\copy rescan_queries(id, embedding) FROM '__QUERIES_FILE__' WITH (FORMAT csv)
+
+CREATE INDEX rescan_items_ivfflat_idx
+ON rescan_items
+USING ivfflat (embedding vector_cosine_ops)
+WITH (lists = __LISTS__);
+
+ANALYZE rescan_items;
+ANALYZE rescan_queries;
+\endif
+
+SET enable_seqscan = off;
+SET enable_bitmapscan = off;
+SET ivfflat.probes = __PROBES__;
+
+\pset tuples_only on
+\pset format unaligned
+\o __PID_FILE__
+SELECT pg_backend_pid();
+\o
+\pset tuples_only off
+\pset format aligned
+
+SELECT pg_sleep(__STARTUP_DELAY__);
+
+EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, COSTS OFF, SUMMARY ON)
+SELECT sum(nn.id::bigint)
+FROM rescan_queries q
+CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
+    SELECT i.id
+    FROM rescan_items i
+    ORDER BY i.embedding <=> q.embedding
+    LIMIT 1
+) nn;
+
+SELECT pg_sleep(__STARTUP_DELAY__);
